#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106598} :activity/id #uuid "f5bc6246-bbe1-5e71-a301-e64564d76448" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049386 :topic/id #uuid "f0b09481-01c6-54c3-9c3a-c58994bdbc06" :topic/math-academy-id 1281} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107150 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/3538fb94de9aac062e7ed65b9496085d478be911e5da2bf8480b260cec70ae4f.png)\n\nWe can approximate the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1$.\n\n![](images/cb/cbb7b5af27aa5ebd3b5bbb51026017454999682dcf7e172411df79278564a5ae.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(2)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(3)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle) + [f(4)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]3[/math]rd rectangle); = [2^{2} \\cdot 1] + [3^{2} \\cdot 1] + [4^{2} \\cdot 1]; = 4 + 9 + 16; = 29\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **right Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*right* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4]$, the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area (see the picture above)." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8dc0c69-e0be-5d9c-bb2a-4838709fb9eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 733 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "39a0db46-10ca-5ca8-80cd-7262b7bef7bb" :step/math-academy-id 1411 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105577 :question/id #uuid "2eb23241-bd2b-5b76-b885-25cd3a51c16d" :question/math-academy-id "e-392"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235418}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "5ad9803e-7cb4-50f5-8c8e-7a9260fa6ed5" :step/math-academy-id 1412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107151 :tutorial/content "In general, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ can be approximated using a right Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/6a/6aa3fd6dca84719aa7d34fdf75586a33005dd34c416d4456662f9d1876679f61.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx f(x_1)\\Delta x+f(x_2)\\Delta x+f(x_3)\\Delta x+ \\cdots + f(x_n) \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles and the step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the $\\Delta x$, which gives the following general formula:\n\n$$\nheights\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09dcf1d5-eddf-58d0-8f4b-eeae9cf26024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 735 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "4b4ff77f-3654-5883-84bd-bc2a0712cbe5" :step/math-academy-id 1415 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106599}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107152 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta{x}$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nThe first thing we note is that the domain of the function $y=f(x)$ is *partitioned* as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} [1,4] &= [1, 1.5] \\cup [1.5, 2.5] \\cup [2.5, 4]. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/60/60c0bfba52fe087b4d8aaeceee367798e24079dc385afff3e83bca60405d4d2b.png)\n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our right Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the right endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\mathcal{A} &\\approx {\\color{red}2} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}1.5-1}) + {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2.5-1.5}) + {\\color{red}8} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-2.5}) \\\\ &= {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} +{\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1}+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5} \\\\ &=1+6+12 \\\\ &= 19. \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b662a09b-80ce-5d1d-8950-1d407892ead3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 736 :tutorial/title "Right Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "7d5f671d-e0d4-5b0a-8317-1304ba7d929f" :step/math-academy-id 1416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105578 :question/id #uuid "41e80d7f-8a72-5bbf-9f66-c0047b28e45b" :question/math-academy-id "e-394"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372981}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "e15fac05-66ad-5f49-9bed-51db8cc7f71c" :step/math-academy-id 1420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105648 :question/id #uuid "c1deb898-8696-537f-8fb2-ae0f354dec29" :question/math-academy-id "e-7231"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354467}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "f11aa5d6-b2b3-51c0-8bd1-0228ad7b133b" :step/math-academy-id 26749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107100 :tutorial/content "Whether the right Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.![](images/71/71bc3147a2d7ee5867740e67f55d2a257065f0f225b62b4af3b5f5990c0d4ffc.png)\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/0b/0bc5bc2d5daa2b42e640d31b998707af503b4c4489531ef7ddd41f4374405ef7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca191bef-f207-5cb0-88a1-6c46288c5c09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4373 :tutorial/title "Overestimates and Underestimates"} :step/id #uuid "efefd8b4-c7ee-50d3-abef-483a7d25557b" :step/math-academy-id 26751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105649 :question/id #uuid "1f74cbf1-c3f5-5ac2-ad55-ad22d7dae3ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386785}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"} :step/id #uuid "a49d1998-d787-5d44-a81a-b54f11630c6b" :step/math-academy-id 26752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106601}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas With the Right Riemann Sum" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105959}]}
